lesson k-number-bonds-to-five v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=3 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Number bonds to five"
  goal "Find the missing part when five is split into two parts."

  look {
    scene 14x7 {
      tenframe f count=3 color=berry at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
        Five is the top row of the frame. Three counters and two empty squares: three and two make
        five. Every way of splitting five into two parts is a number bond to five.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.bond-five-frame n=4 level=medium
    }
    practice k.bond-five-frame count=2 seed=2
    practice k.bond-under-cup count=2 seed=3
    practice k.bond-fingers count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    practice k.bond-pairs count=2 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.bond-swap
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.bond-all-ways k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Five is two parts: 4 and 1, 3 and 2, and turned round."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. The ten frame lesson read a total; this one is the step on from it,
    finding a part when the whole is known. Fingers on one hand are the best tool there is. If the
    child is stuck, put five buttons on the table and hide some under a cup. Off the page, share
    five grapes between two plates every way you can. England, Reception, ELG Number: "Have a deep
    understanding of number to 10, including the composition of each number".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level four and one is worked in the teacher's pen first, the missing parts are one or two, and the ways to share four are counted."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the missing parts are three or four, the pairs include five and zero, and the ways to share five are counted."
  }
}
